Membership information = knowledge about which nodes currently belong to the cluster and their status.

Example:

Cluster has:

N1 → UP
N2 → UP
N3 → DOWN
N4 → UP

Each node may maintain information like:

Node ID    IP        Status
N1         10.0.0.1  UP
N2         10.0.0.2  UP
N3         10.0.0.3  DOWN
N4         10.0.0.4  UP

This is membership information.

How does Gossip help?

N1 checks N3 and finds it isn't responding:

N1 → N3: "Are you alive?"
N3 → No response

N1 suspects N3 is down.

Then N1 tells other nodes:

N1 → N2: "I think N3 is down."
N2 → N4: "N3 seems to be down."

Eventually, most nodes learn:

N3 = DOWN

This is gossip-based failure detection.




The client sends the request to any available node. 
That node becomes the coordinator. 
The coordinator uses the partitioning algorithm to determine the replica set for the key. 
If one of the intended replicas is unavailable, the coordinator temporarily stores the write on another available node 
with a hint identifying the intended replica. 

When the failed replica recovers, the temporary node forwards the data to it.


The coordinator doesn't necessarily have to be N1 because N1 owns the data. 
N1 is simply the node that received the request. 
Any node can act as coordinator in a leaderless architecture.




