Anti-Entropy.
* When a previously failed server rejoins, it is missing updates that happened while it was down.
* Other nodes detect this discrepancy and initiate a state synchronization.
* The updates are gossiped back to the recovered node to bring it up to date.


Node N1                         Node N2
   Root Hash                      Root Hash
      /   \                          /   \
     A     B                        A     B
    / \   / \                      / \   / \
   A1 A2 B1 B2                    A1 A2 B1 B2
Step 1: Compare root
Root(N1) == Root(N2)

Then:

All data represented by this tree is the same.
Nothing needs to be synchronized.

Step 2: Root is different
Root(N1) != Root(N2)

Go down the tree:

       Root ❌
       /    \
      A      B
     ✅     ❌

We know:

Left subtree A → same
Right subtree B → different

So we don't need to inspect/synchronize A.

Step 3: Continue only with different subtree
       B ❌
      /   \
    B1     B2
    ✅     ❌

Now we know only B2 contains differences.

Eventually, we identify the specific data range/partition that differs.

N1: B2 → old data
N2: B2 → newer data

Then synchronize only that range instead of comparing the entire dataset.



